        }
      ],
      "impact": "The fan power pin is shorted to the potentiometer wiper and MCU ADC input. When Q1 turns on, 12V (VCC) is applied to the pot and MCU, likely damaging them. Even when Q1 is off, the fan cannot draw power from the pot wiper, so the fan will not run properly.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["JP1"],
      "pins": ["JP1.2"],
      "nets": ["VCC", "+5V"],
      "observed": "JP1.2 is connected to the VCC net (unregulated input, likely 12V) instead of the +5V net.",
      "expected": "JP1.2 should be connected to +5V, because the potentiometer is powered from +5V (J3.1 on +5V) and the jumper is intended to provide a stable voltage to the POT input.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "VCC : C1.1(passive) J5.2(Pin_2,passive) JP1.2(B,passive) Q1.2(S,passive) R1.1(passive) R6.1(passive) U1.3(VI,power_in)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "+5V : C2.1(passive) J3.1(Pin_1,passive) J4.2(VCC,passive) J6.2(Pin_2,passive) R3.1(passive) U1.2(VO,power_out) U2.8(VCC,power_in) U3.8(VCC,power_in)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If the jumper is closed, 12V (VCC) is applied to the potentiometer wiper and MCU ADC pin, exceeding their voltage ratings and causing damage.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain two anomalies: the fan power pin (J2.2) is shorted to the potentiometer wiper and MCU ADC input on the POT net, and the VCC_STABLE jumper (JP1.2) is connected to the unregulated VCC rail instead of the regulated +5V rail. Both would cause malfunction and likely damage the MCU or potentiometer."
}

